From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Romeu Mello <romeumello@icloud.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 00:40:27 +0900
Subject: unix-slow-reader: the unix proxy never blocks the vsock thread on a
 host peer that stops reading

## Why virtx needs it

A published port reaches the guest through a unix socket libkrun listens on (`add_unix_port`), relayed from the port's listener by virtx-uvm/src/machine/net/relay.rs.
Upstream writes guest data to that socket with a blocking `send()` on the thread that serves every vsock connection, so a client that stops reading one connection stops every other port and the guest's network with it.
virtx-rs/tests/ports.rs `a_port_carries_a_large_answer_to_a_reader_that_falls_behind` fails without it.

## What it changes

libkrun#978 (https://github.com/libkrun/libkrun/pull/978), its last two commits as they are, less the test they add to libkrun's own suite; its first commit is libkrun#973, carried as unix-half-close.
The socket stays non-blocking: what it does not take waits in a buffer of the connection's own, and the credit returned to the guest advances only as bytes reach the host.
On macOS, deleting an fd from the epoll deletes each kqueue filter in its own call, so a WRITE filter is not left behind when the READ one was never registered.

## When it can go

When libkrun#978 is merged.
